@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# Audio pacing during recovery
+
+Previously the queue allowance changed from 120 ms at any stretched tempo to 80 ms at exactly normal tempo. A recovering game could therefore spend another 40 ms draining audio even when it had CPU headroom. The allowance now approaches the normal limit continuously as tempo recovers.
+
+The virtual-time regression first runs a slow producer and then gives it ample CPU headroom. Near normal tempo, the original code inserts a 42 ms frame with 1024-frame device blocks and a 53 ms frame with 512-frame blocks. The continuous allowance keeps that maximum at 22 ms for both block sizes, without starving the device. Early recovery still legitimately follows the slower audio clock; this change does not force audio/video out of sync.
+
+The macOS host also requests 512-frame callbacks (10.67 ms at 48 kHz) to reduce pacing granularity. Other platforms retain 1024. The host logs the obtained buffer size because the driver may adjust it. [SDL documents buffer sizing and its latency tradeoff](https://wiki.libsdl.org/SDL2/SDL_OpenAudioDevice).
+
+Considered alternatives were smaller device blocks, a continuous queue allowance, pacing to a wall clock, and asynchronous unstretched output. The first two preserve the existing audio-clock synchronization and pitch-preserving stretcher; the latter two change synchronization behavior and were not selected.
+
+Validation: the added recovery checks fail against the original header (40/42 virtual-time checks pass) and pass with the fix. The full stretcher/pipe suite passes 99/99 checks, including pitch, continuity, sustained slow production, a long stall, flushing on load and the stretch limit. Alternating 700-frame saved-state SC3 runs obtained 512-frame callbacks and had no dry queue or dropped samples. Aggregate run-time differences were within run-to-run variation; the isolated recovery test establishes the specific pacing fix, not a general FPS increase.
